    <item>
      <title>Wine 11.22 Introduces x86 AVX Emulation On ARM64</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Wine-11.22-Released</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Wine-11.22-Released</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 14 Nov 2026 01:35:04 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="WINE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/wine.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Wine 11.22 is out today as the latest bi-weekly development release and inching quite closely toward the Wine 12.0 stable release preparations. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Most notable with Wine 11.22 is adding Advanced Vector Extensions (AVX) support in the x86 emulation path on Wine ARM64. For helping with modern Windows software assuming AVX support, it can now be emulated nicely with ARM64EC builds. This follows the &lt;a href="https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/merge_requests/12610"&gt;initial NTDLL for AVX on ARM64EC&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/merge_requests/12683"&gt;other changes&lt;/a&gt; around the XState handling. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Wine 11.22 also updates the locale data to Unicode CLDR 49, adds address space randomization, improves the glyph metrics for the Tahoma font, DTD validation is re-implemented in XML, and the usual assortment of bug fixes. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;For Wine 11.22 there are 34 known bug fixes in this release ranging from the Logos Bible Software to Guild Wars 2 to Star Citizen and various other game and app fixes. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;All the Wine 11.22 release details can be found at &lt;a href="https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/releases/wine-11.22"&gt;WineHQ.org&lt;/a&gt;..&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Cloudflare Open Sources Kitesurf: Their Web Browser For AI Agents</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Cloudflare-Kitesurf-Open-Source</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Cloudflare-Kitesurf-Open-Source</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 13 Nov 2026 21:07:09 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="FREE SOFTWARE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/freesoftware.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Earlier this year Cloudflare announced Kitesurf as a stateless web browser for AI agents. After building out its features, they have now made good on their word to open-source it. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Kitesurf is now open-source under an Apache 2.0 license for this web browser built for agents. Kitesurf is built atop Cloudflare Workers and can parse HTML, handle CSS, and execute JavaScript all without a browser contianer. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=kitesurf" alt="Kitesurf" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;As for Cloudflare's expressed motivation with Kitesurf: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"Our main goal is to give AI agents a lightweight, efficient browser — one that can load websites, execute JavaScript, and interact with pages while using less CPU and memory than a full browser like Chromium, and scaling on demand across Cloudflare’s global Workers network."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Those interested can read today's &lt;a href="https://blog.cloudflare.com/kitesurf-open-source/"&gt;open-source announcement&lt;/a&gt; for Kitesurf. The Kitesurf code is available via &lt;a href="https://github.com/cloudflare/kitesurf"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;.  More details on Kitesurf itself can be found at the project site of &lt;a href="https://kitesurf.dev/"&gt;kitesurf.dev&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>AMD openSIL Achieves Its Production Goal With 6th Gen EPYC "Venice"</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMD-openSIL-Venice-Goal</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMD-openSIL-Venice-Goal</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 13 Nov 2026 18:20:29 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="AMD" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/amd.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Since 2023 I and many open-source advocates have been very eager for AMD's &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/OpenSIL"&gt;openSIL&lt;/a&gt; project for delivering &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMD-openSIL-Open-Source"&gt;open-source CPU silicon initialization&lt;/a&gt; as &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMD-openSIL-September-2024"&gt;a replacement to the traditional AGESA code&lt;/a&gt;. With Zen 5 there was proof-of-concept support that we have enjoyed with Coreboot on &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/review/msi-b850p-coreboot-opensil"&gt;an MSI Ryzen motherboard&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/review/amd-opensil-coreboot-ar1"&gt;a Gigabyte EPYC server motherboard&lt;/a&gt; thanks to the Coreboot-based Dasharo ports by 3mdeb. The goal for production readiness was for Zen 6, but with this week's EPYC 9006 SP7 announcement the openSIL topic wasn't touched on. So I asked around.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;With the AMD EPYC 9006 series SP7 processors now shipping, capturing the headlines has been the incredible Zen 6 server performance with the likes of the &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/review/amd-epyc-9686f"&gt;EPYC 9686F&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/review/amd-epyc-9996"&gt;EPYC 9996&lt;/a&gt; processors. In the media briefings and slides, openSIL wasn't touched on that piqued my curiosity whether everything was still on track per their earlier goals, what was happening in terms of the CPU silicon initialization for Venice, etc.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;I followed up with AMD and, fortunately, all is well for openSIL. OpenSIL achieved its previously-stated production goals with 6th Gen EPYC "Venice". Wondering if the openSIL path was optional and perhaps the classic AGESA path also still supported/used, I was relayed as part of the messaging, "&lt;em&gt;no Venice platform has booted anything without openSIL&lt;/em&gt;."  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=2026&amp;image=venice_opensil_1_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=venice_opensil_1_med" alt="AMD EPYC Nigeria server" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;It was also reaffirmed that they will be releasing the open-source code to openSIL for Venice within one quarter of the official product launch. So the openSIL Venice code should be out within roughly three months, aligning with their earlier openSIL presentations.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;It looks like all of their &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMD-openSIL-September-2024"&gt;openSIL goals laid out in 2024&lt;/a&gt; are all in good shape and being achieved with Venice as planned:  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=2024&amp;image=opensil_osfc_2_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2024&amp;image=opensil_osfc_2_med" alt="AMD openSIL Roadmap" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=2024&amp;image=opensil_osfc_6_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2024&amp;image=opensil_osfc_6_med" alt="AMD openSIL Zen 6" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Great to see everything aligned and a relief after the lack of any openSIL messaging around the EPYC 9006 SP7 shipping.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;As for any Coreboot-based EPYC 9006 servers, AMD is continuing to monitor customer support/interest. At the very least once the openSIL Venice code is out there in a quarter, it opens up the possibility like what we saw with 3mdeb's work on them &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/review/gigabyte-mz33-ar1"&gt;independently bringing their Coreboot-based Dasharo to retail motherboards&lt;/a&gt;.  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=2026&amp;image=venice_opensil_2_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=venice_opensil_2_med" alt="AMD EPYC Nigeria server with OpenBMC" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Lastly, for those wondering, the AMD EPYC 9006 reference motherboards continue making use of OpenBMC. AMD &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/review/amd-genoa-openbmc"&gt;began using OpenBMC on their reference boards with Genoa&lt;/a&gt; and always warms the heart seeing it used over proprietary BMC software alternatives.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>KDE Frameworks 6.32 Released With That Fix For USB File Transfer Notifications</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/KDE-Frameworks-6.32</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/KDE-Frameworks-6.32</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 13 Nov 2026 17:02:33 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="KDE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/kde.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; KDE Frameworks 6.32 shipped today as the newest monthly update to this set of KDE components. Notable this month is a fix for the previously-reported 15 year old bug around inaccurate notifications for USB file transfers. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Found in KDE Frameworks 6.32 is a fix for &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/KDE-Fixes-15-Year-Old-Bugs"&gt;inaccurate notifications when copying files to a removable USB flash drive&lt;/a&gt;. There would be a "completion" notification the past 15 years that only reflected when copying to the local cache and not when the data is actually written out to the removable storage. With today's KDE Frameworks 6.32 that is now resolved by leveraging the sync_file_range system call. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=kde_copy_2" alt="KDE copy dialog" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;KDE Frameworks 6.32 also brings many Baloo fixes, KArchive fixes, Kirigami updates, syntax highlighting adjustments, and other changes. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=cachyos-omarchy-benchmarks&amp;image=omarchy_cachyos_arch_3_med" alt="CachyOS KDE Plasma desktop" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Downloads and more details on the KDE Frameworks 6.32 update via &lt;a href="https://kde.org/announcements/frameworks/6/6.32.0/"&gt;KDE.org&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>AMD Leverages AI + HRX Loom In Experiment For Quickly Porting 50 LLMs To Client GPUs</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMD-HRX-Loom-Experiment</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMD-HRX-Loom-Experiment</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 13 Nov 2026 15:53:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="AMD" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/amd.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Following the recent release of &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMD-ROCm-HRX-Loom-0.1"&gt;HRX Loom v0.1&lt;/a&gt; and leveraging an AI agent workflow, a team at AMD experimented with bringing 50 trending Hugging Face models over to AMD client GPUs. They accomplished this feat in just around three days, thanks to their HRX software work paired with the power of Ryzen AI Max "Strix Halo" hardware.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;AMD engineers Jeremy Fowers and Ben Vanik led an experiment to bring 50 popular Hugging Face models across 18 architectures over to AMD client GPUs in just three to four days. This experiment was done to show the potential of bringing new as well as niche models to AMD hardware faster thanks to HRX Loom and agentic AI.  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=hrx" alt="AMD HRX diagram" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;They also found that there was a medium 1.5~2.1x speed-up for 32K token prompt processing compared to Llama.cpp's ROCm back-end. Compared to Llama.cpp's Vulkan back-end was a 1.9x speed-up. HRX Loom remains an experimental project for now at AMD but showing great potential given these results. HRX Loom is not only for GPUs but also supports CPU and NPU execution too.  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=hrx_experiment_med" alt="AMD HRX experiment results" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Those curious about this AMD HRX Loom experiment can find their results via &lt;a href="https://loom-fun.github.io/loom-skills/"&gt;the Loom-Fun GitHub page&lt;/a&gt;.  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=2026&amp;image=hrx_halo_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=hrx_halo_med" alt="Strix Halo systems" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;As part of this work is the &lt;a href="https://github.com/loom-fun/loom-skills"&gt;loom-skills&lt;/a&gt; deliverable for equipping Claude Code, Codex, or Pi agent with the necessary bits to author and serve optimized model programs to AMD client GPUs -- with the current focus/testing being on the wonderful &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/Strix+Halo"&gt;Strix Halo&lt;/a&gt; hardware with the likes of the &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/Ryzen+AI+Halo"&gt;Ryzen AI Halo&lt;/a&gt; or &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/Framework+Desktop"&gt;Framework Desktop&lt;/a&gt;. More background information on AMD's Loom can be found via the &lt;a href="https://rocm.github.io/hrx-system/loom/"&gt;documentation&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Linux 7.5 To Include New hwpoison-panic Tool For Intentionally Crashing Your System</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.5-hwpoison-panic</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.5-hwpoison-panic</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 13 Nov 2026 15:26:14 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="LINUX KERNEL" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/linuxkernel.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; A new tool expected to be merged to the Linux 7.5 source tree provides a new exercise for the kernel and in the process bringing down the system. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The new &lt;strong&gt;hwpoison-panic&lt;/strong&gt; tool is set to be added to the Linux kernel source tree that tests out the  &lt;em&gt;vm.panic_on_unrecoverable_memory_failure&lt;/em&gt; option for forcing an immediate kernel panic when an unrecoverable memory failures hits specific unrecoverable kernel-owned page types. This tool makes it easy to test the kernel behavior without waiting for multi-bit ECC errors on kernel-owned pages or other similar hardware memory errors. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The hwpoison-panic tool to intentionally trigger the kernel panic injects a hardware poison error on a kernel owned page with the ability to target the kernel read-only data "rodata" range, a slab page, or a page-table page. Successfully running the tool will crash the running kernel and thus the &lt;em&gt;--yes-panic-my-kernel&lt;/em&gt; argument is also needed to avoid accidentally triggering this tool. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=hwpoison_panic" alt="hwpoison-panic" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Testing with this tool is primarily intended for VMs where the serial console can also be captured for confirming the kernel panic and system crash. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Those curious about this hwpoison-panic Linux tool can find the details via &lt;a href="https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/mm/linux.git/commit/?h=for-next&amp;id=0a936c44f2dcfbc95ffcdec30b04cc9a59c84e86"&gt;this patch&lt;/a&gt; queued into Andrew Morton's MM Git "for-next" branch ahead of Linux 7.5.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>AMD AOCL 6.0 Introduces New RNG Library, Initial Zen 6 CPU Optimizations</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMD-AOCL-6.0</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMD-AOCL-6.0</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 13 Nov 2026 14:43:17 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="AMD" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/amd.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; In addition to &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMD-AOCC-6.0-Released"&gt;AOCC 6.0&lt;/a&gt; releasing this week as their updated C/C++ compiler along with the new "AOF" Fortran compiler, AMD also released AOCL 6.0 as the AMD Optimizing CPU Libraries. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Now that the AMD EPYC 9006 SP7 processors are shipping, AMD has been rolling out their updated optimized CPU software for Zen 6. With AOCL 6.0 comes their Zen 6 CPU optimizations while also bringing some optimizations for prior-generation EPYC/Ryzen Zen CPUs. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;AOCL-BLAS has tuning for Zen 6 plus various kernel optimizations going back to help with Zen 4 and Zen 5 performance. AOCL Cryptography and Data Analytics components also have Zen 6 optimizations while AOCL-DLP has INT8 improvements for Zen 6. The AOCL-LibMem library brings optimized memory and string routines for Zen 6. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=amd-epyc-9996&amp;image=amd_epyc_9996_2_med" alt="AMD EPYC Venice Zen 6 CPU" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;A new library with AOCL 6.0 is AOCL-OpenRNG. AOCL-OpenRNG is an open-source random number generator library optimized for Zen processors. This includes AVX2-optimized kernels, 11 base generators, 17 statistical distributions, and supporting both Windows and Linux systems. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Some of the other changes include a Threading Building Blocks "TBB" back-end for AOCL-Compression, compile-time ISA enablement in the compression code, encryption/decryption improvements, API hardening to AOCL-FFTW, and new APIs too for AOCL-LAPACK. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Downloads and more details on the AMD Optimizing CPU Libraries 6.0 release via &lt;a href="https://www.amd.com/en/developer/aocl.html"&gt;AMD.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>XRAID Publicly Released As New RAID Implementation For XFS</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/XRAID-XFS-RAID</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/XRAID-XFS-RAID</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 13 Nov 2026 13:22:22 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="LINUX STORAGE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/linuxstorage.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Longtime Linux developer Christoph Hellwig today announced the initial public release of XRAID. XRAID is a declustered parity RAID implementation integrated into the XFS file-system. XRAID is focused on RAID for zoned XFS deployments. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Hellwig explained of this new XRAID functionality for XFS: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"The two primary aims of XRAID are to support parity RAID on zoned devices like SMR hard drives, and to provide a fast RAID implementation without inherent data integrity problems.  XRAID, just like zoned XFS also works on conventional hard drives and SSDs.  Optimizing for SSD performance has not been a focus, but even without that, writes are up to 20 times faster than mdraid. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;XRAID builds on the zoned allocator, which uses the RT subvolume to store file data separately from metadata, so that only file data is placed on sequential write required zones while metadata is stored on conventional devices or zones. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;XRAID takes advantage of this organization to only provide parity RAID for file data, where parity RAID works efficiently.  Metadata redundancy is delegated to existing stacked Linux drivers like MD RAID mirroring, which is more suitable than parity RAID for small objects that are updated in place. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The RAID implementation sits below the Zoned XFS I/O path, similar to a stacked block driver, and aggregates the physical zones of the underlying devices into logical zones that are used by the Zoned XFS as realtime groups. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Declustered RAID describes a variant of RAID that distributes the D + P data and parity blocks in each stripe across a storage pool that contains more than D + P disks so that all disks participate in read, write and reconstruction operations and spare capacity is distributed over all disks. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Declustered RAID provides much faster rebuild times than conventional RAID, and eases using of a large number of disks in a single storage pool. XRAID can rebuild to all devices that have spare capacity, and the rebuild order is dynamic, allowing to rebuild zones with multiple lost redundancies first, and rebuilding data zones before parity zones to reduce the overhead of degraded reads."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;XRAID has been in development for a year and a half. The initial public patches aren't yet ready for upstream submission but now at least at a stage for more testing and feedback. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The kernel patches are out for review on &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/20261113112913.GA31748@lst.de/"&gt;the kernel mailing list&lt;/a&gt;. There is also a branch of the xfsprogs user-space programs for mkfs support with XRAID as well as a new "xraid" utility. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=xraid_1" alt="XRAID" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Earlier this year Hellwig presented on the XRAID development and those interested can find his prior slides &lt;a href="https://verein.lst.de/~hch/raid-sucks.pdf"&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=xraid_2" alt="XRAID benchmarks" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The performance of XRAID is looking good in the early benchmarks shown in that presentation.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Linux 7.5 To Upstream Driver For New Glanda GPU</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.5-GlandaGPU-Driver</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.5-GlandaGPU-Driver</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 13 Nov 2026 12:55:37 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="LINUX KERNEL" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/linuxkernel.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Sent out today was the drm-misc-next pull request for the week of new material to queue in DRM-Next until the Linux 7.5 merge window gets underway in late December. Most notable this week is the introduction of the new GlandaGPU driver. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;GlandaGPU was covered a few months ago on Phoronix as &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/GlandaGPU-Linux-Driver-RFC"&gt;an open-source soft GPU core design&lt;/a&gt;. GlandaGPU is designed in VHDL and running currently on FPGA hardware such as the Cycle V FPGA SoC. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;It's an open-source, community-based GPU design that can also be emulated with QEMU for those lacking the FPGA hardware. It's fun for learning and open-source demos but don't expect to do any gaming or productive tasks on it with its VGA output at 640x480 and 2D acceleration engine. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=glanda_gpu" alt="GlandaGPU" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;In addition to queuing this GlandaGPU driver for the mainline Linux kernel, this week also brought HyperV cursor plane support, support for the Allwinner T527 VIP9000 in the Etnaviv driver, and various panel updates. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The full list of drm-misc-next patches this week for Linux 7.5 can be found via &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/dri-devel/48d133a9-097f-4be3-b50e-66a703e57a2c@linux.intel.com/"&gt;this pull request&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Intel Posts Initial GCC Compiler Code For Razor Lake &amp; Coral Rapids</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Intel-GCC-Coral-Rapids-Razor</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Intel-GCC-Coral-Rapids-Razor</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 13 Nov 2026 11:29:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="INTEL" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/intel.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; One of the consistent wonderful praise I have for Intel over the past 22+ years of covering the Linux hardware scene has been their upstreaming of new CPU targets into the open-source compilers like GCC well ahead of product launches. Particularly with GCC on an annual feature release cycle and not being quickly picked up by new Linux distribution releases, Intel upstreaming their new CPU targets and ISA features at times years in advance of product releases has helped ensure smooth compiler ecosystem support by the time their products ship. The latest example, today they posted their patches providing new ISA features and enabling for both Coral Rapids and Razor Lake processors.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Since late 2024 there was &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Intel-Diamond-Rapids-March-GCC"&gt;GCC support for Diamond Rapids&lt;/a&gt; as the next-generation Xeon processors launching in 2027. Now even before Diamond Rapids is shipping, the initial Coral Rapids support is now in patch form and will be presumably merged in time for the GCC 17.1 compiler release due out in early 2027. Similarly, Intel &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Intel-Nova-Lake-GCC-16"&gt;Nova Lake support came to GCC 16 last year&lt;/a&gt; and Razor Lake as its successor is out today with these new GCC patches.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Coral Rapids and Razor Lake aren't shipping until 2028 at least but this very early GCC enablement should be in place for GCC 17 and work its way out as the default compiler in various Linux distributions by the time these processors are shipping to customers a year later. Kudos to Intel for again delivering their compiler patches so early.  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=gcc_coral_razor_med" alt="Razor Lake and Coral Rapids GCC patches" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;The Razor Lake &lt;a href="https://gcc.gnu.org/pipermail/gcc-patches/2026-November/738673.html"&gt;patch&lt;/a&gt; confirms that Razor Lake has all the same ISA features as Nova Lake. So not particularly interesting there from the ISA perspective.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;The &lt;a href="https://gcc.gnu.org/pipermail/gcc-patches/2026-November/738674.html"&gt;patch&lt;/a&gt; bringing up Coral Rapids is more interesting and confirms the ISA capabilities of:  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"Intel Coral Rapids CPU with 64-bit extensions, MOVBE, MMX, SSE, SSE2, SSE3, SSSE3, SSE4.1, SSE4.2, POPCNT, CX16, SAHF, FXSR, AVX, XSAVE, PCLMUL, FSGSBASE, RDRND, F16C, AVX2, BMI, BMI2, LZCNT, FMA, MOVBE, HLE, RDSEED, ADCX, PREFETCHW, AES, CLFLUSHOPT, XSAVEC, XSAVES, SGX, AVX512F, AVX512VL, AVX512BW, AVX512DQ, AVX512CD, PKU, AVX512VBMI, AVX512IFMA, SHA, AVX512VNNI, GFNI, VAES, AVX512VBMI2, VPCLMULQDQ, AVX512BITALG, RDPID, AVX512VPOPCNTDQ, PCONFIG, WBNOINVD, CLWB, MOVDIRI, MOVDIR64B, ENQCMD, CLDEMOTE, PTWRITE, WAITPKG, SERIALIZE, TSXLDTRK, UINTR, AMX-BF16, AMX-TILE, AMX-INT8, AVX-VNNI, AVX512FP16, AVX512BF16, AMX-FP16, PREFETCHI, AMX-COMPLEX, AVX10.1, AVX-IFMA, AVX-NE-CONVERT, AVX-VNNI-INT16, AVX-VNNI-INT8, CMPccXADD, SHA512, SM3, SM4, AVX10.2, APX_F, AMX-AVX512, AMX-FP8, AMX-TF32, MOVRS, AMX-MOVRS, AVX10_VPMLDSABFLYDQ instruction set support."&lt;/blockquote&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;AVX10_VPMLDSABFLYDQ being one of the main ISA differences for Coral Rapids over Diamond Rapids. VPMLDSABFLYDQ is for Packed ML-DSA Butterfly Operations. A separate patch today adds the AVX10_VPMLDSABFLYDQ intrinsics for GCC.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Interestingly at least for now Coral Rapids is AVX10.2 confirmed and not AVX10.3. &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/ACE-v2-AVX10.3-Posted"&gt;AVX10.3 specs&lt;/a&gt; were recently posted but looks like that won't be here for Coral Rapids in 2028 nor ACEv2 as presumably the Coral Rapids design was too far along by the time those new specs solidified. Today also marked &lt;a href="https://gcc.gnu.org/pipermail/gcc-patches/2026-November/738680.html"&gt;an updated patch series&lt;/a&gt; for GCC of that AVX10.3 support and also now includes AVX10_BMM support that was left out of the original version.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
